  "worked_solution": "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+2b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $2b.$ So, we first draw the vector $2b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/a8309dc45ac43c6642010e744de5a062d6575d48ba85bd642df39dd261f47422.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $2b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $\u25b3OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=2|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-2a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =2b-2a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+2b=-2a+2b.$\n\nSince $a\u2226b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-2\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
